<dt>Technical checks</dt><dd>Whether AI crawlers can reach the site, and whether your structured data is correct. Faults go to <a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">technical SEO</a>.</dd>
<dt>Authority plan</dt><dd>Where your business should be mentioned and is not.</dd>
<dt>A repeat check</dt><dd>So you can compare.</dd>
</dl>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'How we do it, step by step',
      html: `<ol class="bf-steps">
<li>List the questions a buyer asks before choosing a business like yours.</li>
<li>Record what the AI tools answer today.</li>
<li>Compare those answers with your pages. Find the gaps and the errors.</li>
<li>Fix the pages and the technical access.</li>
<li>Work on mentions elsewhere.</li>
<li>Check again and report visits and enquiries.</li>
</ol>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'What it can and cannot do',
      html: `<p>It can make your business easier for these tools to describe accurately. It cannot guarantee that a tool will mention or link to you: the answers vary from one day to the next and nobody outside those companies controls them. We report what we observe, with dates. Our article on <a href="/blog/getting-cited-in-google-ai-overviews-eligibility-vs-control/">getting cited in Google AI Overviews</a> explains what is and is not within a site owner's control.</p>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'Published examples',
      html: `<p>We have published five reviews of AI referral reports. Each shows which pages AI tools sent visitors to and what we recommended. They are report reviews, not proof that a particular edit caused growth.</p>
